    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Dry farming on the Anatolian plateau",
    text: "Unlike the river lands, central Anatolia farmed by rain. Emmer, barley, lentils and flax were sown in autumn and harvested in early summer, with oxen pulling simple ard ploughs over fields fallowed every other year. The Hittite Laws set prices for everything a farmer dealt in: a plough ox, a field of a given size, a day's hire of a harvester or ox team. Land was often held from the king or a temple in return for service, called sahhan and luzzi, a duty of labour and dues that went with the field and passed to whoever took it over. Short summers and harsh winters made each harvest precarious, and the plateau's grain fed Hattusa's huge silos.",
