    title: "Springs, ponds and the reservoirs of Hattusa",
    text: "Hattusa sat on a rocky slope threaded with springs, and its builders dammed and channelled them into a series of great ponds in the upper city, some lined with clay, to supply a capital that had no river. Clay pipes carried water to the palace and temples, and townswomen drew from springs and wells in jars. Springs were sacred in Hittite religion, homes of deities to whom offerings were made; the stone pool at Eflatunpınar, fed by a spring, is carved with gods holding up the sky. Rivers such as the Kızılırmak, the Halys, were crossed by ferry and fished for carp, but fishing was a small part of life on the dry plateau.",
