    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Compensation and the council of elders",
    text: "The Hittite Laws are notable for preferring payment to vengeance. For killing, wounding, theft or trespass they set compensation in silver, sheep or labour, and several clauses record that an old penalty had been reduced: 'formerly they gave thirty shekels, but now the king has made it fifteen.' Quarrels in towns went first to the council of elders, and serious matters to royal officials or the king himself. Instructions to frontier governors tell them to judge fairly, not favour kin, and to restore a man's stolen property. For villagers a dispute over a field, an ox or a runaway servant ended not in punishment but in a payment that set the balance right.",
