    text: "Hittite ritual texts name well over a hundred kinds of bread and cake, loaves shaped as animals, sun discs and hands, sweetened with honey or figs, spiced or mixed with cheese. At home the fare was plainer: barley and emmer bread and porridge, lentils and chickpeas, onions, cheese, grapes and figs, beer and wine, and meat on festival days. Women ground grain, baked, brewed, spun and wove, and the household's servants did the same. Unlike Mesopotamia, the plateau had oak and pine forest, so firewood was cut and carried from the hills, and the long winters made laying in a good woodpile one of the essential tasks of autumn.",
