    text: "A Hittite marriage began with a betrothal payment, the kusata, from the groom to the bride's family; if another man eloped with her, he had to repay it. The bride brought a dowry, which she kept if widowed or divorced. The Laws deal carefully with marriages between free and unfree people, and with a woman who leaves her husband. If a husband died, his brother was to marry the widow, then his father, then his nephew, so that she and her children stayed in his family; no penalty applied. A couple's joint property is mentioned in divorce clauses, where house and children were divided between them, the wife usually taking one child and the husband the rest.",
