    text: "One clause of the Hittite Laws fixes what it cost to have a boy taught a trade: if someone gives a young man to be trained as a carpenter, smith, weaver, leatherworker or fuller, he pays a set fee, and if the teacher makes him an expert, the parent gives the teacher a person as well. Craft skill was a family's gift to a son's future, and a master's reward was measured in hands to help him. Scribes learned at the palace, copying Babylonian and Hittite texts. Most children learned at home, girls grinding and weaving beside their mothers, boys at the plough and with the flocks, and in time took over the family's field and its duties of service.",
