    title: "Cremation and the fourteen days of the royal funeral",
    text: "When a Hittite king or queen died, a fourteen-day rite followed: the body was burned, the bones quenched in wine and oil, wrapped in fine cloth and laid in a stone house, and figures of the dead were fed and honoured. Ordinary people too were often cremated, their ashes placed in urns in cemeteries such as Osmankayası near Hattusa, with food for the journey. The dead joined the ancestors, who received offerings at home. Old parents lived in the house of an heir, and the Laws treat care of them as a family matter; in the rituals, the Old Woman, a healer and ritual expert, shows that age could bring authority as well as need.",
