    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Famine grain and debt bondage in Hatti",
    text: "Hittite peasants borrowed seed and silver from wealthier neighbours, temples and officials, and a man who could not repay might work off the debt or hand over a child or servant as a pledge. Several royal prayers and letters describe famine and plague: King Mursili II blamed a twenty-year plague on his father's broken oaths and begged the gods to lift it. At the end of the thirteenth century BCE the kingdom depended on grain shipped from Egypt and Ugarit, and an Egyptian text says Pharaoh sent grain 'to keep alive the land of Hatti'. Restoring a household after a bad year meant borrowing, paying back in the next harvest, and praying the gods had been appeased.",
