    title: "The thousand gods of Hatti",
    text: "The Hittites spoke of the 'thousand gods' of their land, as they took in the deities of every conquered town. At the head stood the Storm God of Hatti and the Sun Goddess of Arinna. Great festivals, such as the spring AN.TAH.SUM of the crocus and the autumn nuntarriyashas, took the king and queen around the holy towns for weeks. Gods lived in their temples as statues that priests washed, fed and clothed, and a household too had its own god of the hearth. Offerings were loaves, beer, wine and sheep, and a worshipper seeking favour made a vow, promising a gift if the god granted a cure, a son or victory. The open-air sanctuary of Yazılıkaya shows the gods in procession.",
