    title: "Measured fields and the harvest tax",
    text: "In Pharaonic Egypt most land belonged to the crown and to temples such as that of Amun at Karnak, and was worked by farmers who kept part of the crop and paid the rest. Each year after the flood, scribes with measuring cords resurveyed the fields, whose boundaries the water had erased, and set the expected yield; the Wilbour Papyrus of about 1140 BCE lists thousands of plots along a stretch of Middle Egypt, their holders and their dues. At harvest the scribes came back to measure the grain, and tomb paintings, like those of the official Menna at Thebes, show farmers who could not pay being beaten before them. Villagers also owed corvée labour on dykes, canals and royal works.",
    wiki: ["Wilbour Papyrus", "TT69", "Ancient Egyptian agriculture"],
