    text: "Egyptian cattle grazed the marshy pastures of the Delta and were driven back to the estates for counting; a painting from the tomb of Nebamun shows herdsmen bowing before the scribe as the herd files past. Herders lived rough, wading canals with calves on their shoulders, and were mocked by the scribal class for it. Goats, sheep and pigs were kept by villagers. Beyond the valley lay the red land of the desert, where hunters with dogs, nets and bows took gazelle, oryx, ibex, hare and ostrich, and the marshes where men went fowling with throwsticks. Nobles hunted for sport; for others the desert and marsh were extra food and a place of danger.",
    wiki: ["Tomb of Nebamun", "Ancient Egyptian agriculture", "Ancient Egypt"],
