    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Sailing south, drifting north",
    text: "The Nile was Egypt's road. The current carried boats north, and the prevailing wind blew south, so the hieroglyph for travelling upstream was a boat with its sail up and for downstream a boat with the mast down. Barges carried grain, stone and cattle; ferrymen took people across for a fee; small papyrus skiffs served fishermen and fowlers. Overland, donkeys carried goods along the valley edge, and the Middle Kingdom Tale of the Eloquent Peasant begins with a man loading his donkeys with salt, natron and reeds for market, only to be robbed. Letters, royal decrees and gossip travelled by boat, so news reached a village at the pace of the river.",
