    title: "Turquoise, gold and greywacke from the deserts",
    text: "Egypt's stone and metal came from its deserts, reached by royal expeditions of hundreds or thousands of men. At Serabit el-Khadim in Sinai, miners dug turquoise and copper and left inscriptions to Hathor, 'Lady of Turquoise', in a temple they built there. In the Wadi Hammamat, between the Nile and the Red Sea, quarrymen cut the dark greywacke prized for statues, and a papyrus now in Turin, drawn around 1150 BCE, maps the wadi's quarries and gold workings, one of the oldest geological maps known. Granite came from Aswan and limestone from Tura. Expedition leaders recorded water, rations and men lost; the work was hot, thirsty and dangerous.",
