    title: "Ptah's workshops: craftsmen of the temple and palace",
    text: "Most skilled Egyptian craftsmen worked in the workshops of temples, palaces and great estates, which supplied them with gold, cedar, stone and linen and fed them in return. The tomb of the vizier Rekhmire at Thebes shows sculptors, carpenters, jewellers, leatherworkers, brickmakers and metalworkers side by side under the eyes of overseers. Their god was Ptah of Memphis, who shaped the world as a craftsman shapes a statue, and his high priest bore the title 'Greatest of the Directors of Craftsmen'. Skill passed from father to son, and a youth became a master by working beside his elders until an overseer judged him ready; a family might hold the same post for generations.",
