    title: "The local court and the oracle of Amenhotep",
    text: "Egypt was governed through the vizier, provincial officials and scribes, but quarrels were settled close to home. Local courts of officials and respected villagers heard disputes over debts, property and theft. At Deir el-Medina, workers could also bring a question to the oracle of the deified king Amenhotep I, whose statue was carried in procession on priests' shoulders and moved toward 'yes' or 'no', or toward a written answer. Grave matters went higher: the Abbott Papyrus records the investigation of tomb robberies at Thebes under Ramesses IX, with officials accusing each other. The ideal throughout was maat, right order, which a judge was supposed to uphold without favour.",
