    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Mistress of the house: grain, dung fuel and the granary",
    text: "The Egyptian wife was nebet per, 'mistress of the house', in charge of grinding, baking, brewing, spinning and weaving, with daughters and servants beside her. Trees were few in the valley, so cooking fires burned dried dung cakes, straw and chaff, with charcoal for those who could afford it. Grain was kept in mud-brick silos in the courtyard or on the roof, filled through a hatch at the top and emptied from a door below. Wooden models buried with the official Meketre at Thebes show a granary with scribes recording each sack as it was carried in. Going over the stores meant checking for rats and damp, and reckoning how long the grain would last until the next harvest.",
    wiki: ["Meketre", "Ancient Egyptian cuisine", "Ancient Egypt"],
