    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Donkeys on credit at Deir el-Medina",
    text: "The ostraca from Deir el-Medina, the village of the royal tomb builders, are full of loans and unpaid debts: grain lent until the next ration day, a donkey hired and never returned, a coffin painted and not paid for. One workman pursued a debt for a donkey through the local court for years. Debtors swore oaths by Amun and by the ruler to pay by a set day, on pain of beating. When rations came late, as they did under Ramesses III, households borrowed from each other and from officials, and the workmen walked off the job in protest. Recovery from a bad spell depended on neighbours, the next ration and the patience of creditors.",
    wiki: ["Deir el-Medina", "Ostracon", "Ramesses III"],
