    text: "Jerusalem was built of soft white limestone, meleke, quarried from the hills around it and cut with iron picks and wedges, and hardening as it weathered. In the city and towns, potters worked at the wheel in family workshops; Jeremiah goes down to the potter's house and watches him rework a spoiled vessel. The genealogies of Chronicles speak of families of linen workers and of potters who worked for the king, suggesting crafts ran in clans, with sons learning from fathers until they were masters. Skill was seen as a divine gift: in Exodus, Bezalel is filled with the spirit of God in wisdom and understanding to work gold, stone and wood for the Tabernacle.",
    wiki: ["Bezalel", "Meleke", "Kingdom of Judah"],
