    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The tabun, the thorn fire and the royal jars",
    text: "In the four-room house of Iron Age Judah, women ground grain, baked flatbread on the walls of a clay tabun oven, spun and wove on upright looms, and made cheese and yoghurt. Fuel was olive prunings, thorn bushes and dung; Ecclesiastes compares a fool's laughter to the crackle of thorns under a pot. Stores were kept in tall jars in the side rooms: grain, oil, wine, dried figs and raisins. Before the Assyrian attack of 701 BCE, the crown stamped thousands of storage jars with the words lmlk, 'belonging to the king', to stock the fortresses. Going over the stores meant counting jars against the months until harvest.",
    wiki: ["LMLK seal", "Tabun oven", "Four-room house"],
