    text: "Worship in Iron Age Judah was far more varied than the later Bible approves. Beside the Temple in Jerusalem, where priests offered animals, grain and incense to Yahweh, people prayed and sacrificed at local high places, bamot, and at home. Hundreds of small clay figurines of a woman holding her breasts, found in houses, were probably household appeals for fertility and protection, perhaps to Asherah; inscriptions at Kuntillet Ajrud invoke 'Yahweh and his Asherah'. Around 622 BCE King Josiah tried to close the local shrines and centre all sacrifice on Jerusalem. A household seeking help might bring a lamb to the altar, pour a libation, or vow a gift in return for a child or a cure.",
