    text: "Israelite life was organised by the beit av, the 'house of the father', an extended family of three or four generations under its eldest man, often living in adjoining houses. Honouring father and mother was among the ten commandments, and Proverbs warns the son who mocks his father or drives out his mother. The old were fed and kept by their sons, and the elders of a town sat at the gate to judge. Children learned at home: daughters the household crafts, sons farming and herding. Deuteronomy tells parents to repeat the teaching to their children 'when you sit in your house, when you walk by the way, when you lie down and when you rise'.",
