    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Refugees from Samaria on Jerusalem's western hill",
    text: "When Assyria destroyed the northern kingdom of Israel in 722 BCE, refugees poured south. Within a generation Jerusalem grew several times over, spreading from the narrow City of David onto the western hill, where the new quarter was called the Mishneh. King Hezekiah enclosed it with a wall seven metres thick, the Broad Wall, which still cuts through the foundations of houses demolished to make way for it. A couple arriving from Samaria had lost their inherited land and had to build a life from wage work, craft and whatever they had carried, in a city of strangers. Their descendants became Judahites, and some scholars think they brought northern traditions into the scriptures.",
