    title: "Travel rations, darics and silver by weight",
    text: "Darius I struck gold darics and silver sigloi, but in the Persian heartland wages and trade still ran mostly in kind: barley, wine and sheep, with silver weighed out for larger deals, and village barter measured in those units. Travellers on the royal roads carried sealed documents, halmi, entitling them to rations at each waystation, and hundreds of Persepolis tablets record flour and wine issued to parties of workers, officials and caravans on the move. Babylonian houses such as the Murašu family of Nippur lent silver, rented canals and handled crops for landholders, and markets thrived in cities like Susa and Babylon, where coin gradually came into wider use under later dynasties.",
    wiki: ["Daric", "Royal Road", "Persepolis Administrative Archives"],
