    evidence: "documented",
    title: "The king's law and the royal judges",
    text: "The Achaemenid empire was run through satraps, royal secretaries and garrison commanders, with the king's law, data, standing over local custom. Royal judges were appointed for life, and Herodotus tells how Cambyses had a corrupt judge, Sisamnes, flayed and his skin stretched over the judgement seat on which his son was then made to sit. Local disputes stayed with village headmen, priests and elders. Zoroastrian tradition later knew trial by ordeal, var, in which a disputant walked through fire or had molten metal poured on the chest, and under the Sasanians a large body of law, the Book of a Thousand Judgements, recorded rulings on property, marriage and debt.",
    wiki: ["Sisamnes", "Achaemenid Empire", "Sasanian Empire"],
