    text: "Persian households lived on barley and wheat bread, sesame oil, dates, figs, pistachios, curds and cheese, with meat at festivals and wine for those who could get it; Herodotus says Persians ate little at the main meal but many courses of dessert. Stores of grain, wine in jars and dried fruit were kept in storerooms and checked against the lean end of winter, and in the tablets every jar leaving a royal store is recorded. Fuel was scarce on the plateau: wood of tamarisk and thorn scrub, and dung cakes. The hearth fire held special meaning, for Zoroastrians must not pollute fire with dead matter, rubbish or breath, and a family's fire was kept carefully fed.",
