    evidence: "inferred",
    title: "Mud-brick houses, resettled captives and the marriage bond",
    text: "Villagers on the Iranian plateau built with mud brick and pisé on stone footings, roofing with poplar beams and earth that had to be re-rolled after snow. The Persian kings moved whole communities: Herodotus says Darius settled captive Eretrians near Susa, where they worked an oil well, and Sasanian kings built new towns for Roman prisoners. A move meant building a house and breaking new fields under a foreign lord. Marriage was arranged by the families; Zoroastrian teaching made it a religious duty, since children continued the fight of good against evil and kept up rites for the dead. Sasanian law, known from later texts, treated a wife as mistress of the house with rights to support and property.",
