    text: "Herodotus reports that Persian boys were taught from five to twenty just three things: to ride, to draw the bow and to speak the truth. Zoroastrian children, at around fifteen, were invested with the sacred shirt and cord, the sedreh and kusti, and from then on said the prayers and bore responsibility for their deeds. The Persepolis tablets give extra rations to women who had just given birth, double for a boy. Honouring parents was a religious duty, and a son who fed his old parents and made offerings after their death earned merit for all. A widow without a son could have a substitute heir appointed so that the house would not die out.",
