    text: "Herodotus says the Persians held lying to be the most shameful thing, and next to it owing money, because a debtor is bound to tell lies. The inscriptions of Darius name the Lie, drauga, as the enemy of the king's order. In practice, borrowing was common. In Babylonia under the Persians, landholders who owed military service for their 'bow land' borrowed silver from firms like the Murašu of Nippur to pay taxes and equip soldiers, pledging their land and often losing it. In a bad year a peasant household borrowed seed and silver at high interest from a landlord or temple, and could sink into servitude if the next harvest failed as well.",
