    text: "The Persepolis tablets record grain, wine and sheep issued for offerings to Ahuramazda but also to Elamite gods like Humban and to mountains and rivers, made by priests on behalf of the king and the people; religion in early Persia was broader than later Zoroastrian orthodoxy. Households tended the fire, prayed facing light, and offered to the yazatas, the beings worthy of worship, for health and harvest. The Vendidad names three kinds of healer: one who heals with the knife, one with plants, and one with the holy word, and ranks the last highest. A toothache would be treated with herbal pastes and spells in the same breath, and a surgeon had to prove himself on non-believers first.",
