    title: "Punic estates, Numidian herds and elephants for war",
    text: "Carthage's hinterland in what is now Tunisia was farmed as rich estates of grain, olives, vines and fruit, worked by Libyan peasants and slaves under Punic landowners, and the agricultural manual of Mago was prized enough that Rome had it translated. Beyond the estates, the Numidians and Mauri herded horses, cattle, sheep and goats and farmed where rain allowed; King Masinissa pushed his people toward settled farming in the second century BCE. The wild was still wild: Carthage captured North African forest elephants for its armies, and under Rome the province sent lions, leopards and ostriches to the arenas, hunted with nets and pits by specialists.",
