    scope: NAFR,
    topics: [{ workplace: "water" }, { workplace: "workshop" }, { workplace: "extraction" }, { occasion: "day.craft-power" }],
    evidence: "documented",
    title: "Tuna, purple and the quarries of Cap Bon",
    text: "Punic and later Roman North Africa lived by the sea. Fishermen caught tuna in seasonal runs, and coastal works salted fish and fermented it into garum in large vats. Murex shells were crushed for purple dye, a Phoenician skill brought west, and heaps of broken shell mark the dye works at Kerkouane and Djerba. Stone for Carthage came from quarries at El Haouaria on Cap Bon, cut into caves still visible today. In the city, potters, bronzesmiths and jewellers had workshops on the slopes of Byrsa hill. Craftsmen set up stelae in the tophet to Baal Hammon and Tanit, some carved with their tools, in thanks for favours granted.",
