    text: "Roman Africa has left tens of thousands of Latin epitaphs, and they record strikingly old ages, often rounded up: men and women said to have lived eighty, ninety or a hundred years, commemorated by children who had cared for them. Many are raised by a spouse for a partner 'with whom I lived without a quarrel' for so many years. Rome settled veterans in new colonies such as Timgad, founded by Trajan in 100 CE on a grid for retired soldiers of the Third Legion; they received land, married local women, and made new lives far from where they were born. Punic, Libyan and Latin names mix on the stones of such towns.",
    wiki: ["Timgad", "Africa (Roman province)", "Epitaph"],
